Given the following subgraph description loaded from a YAML file, generate
natural language questions that could be answered by querying the full knowledge graph, along with their corresponding
SPARQL queries.

### YAML Subgraph Description:
<Subgraph>
    {{ subgraph_pattern }}
</Subgraph>

Notes on the YAML structure (generic, do not assume any specific KG):
- Each subgraph includes: subgraph_id, num_types, min_support, types, edges, predicate_uris.
- types is a mapping from type label to:
- uri (class URI)
- sample_entities (a few example entity URIs, sometimes with attributes shown as a compact string like attr:type=value)
- edges is a list of triples in a readable form: "TypeA --predicateLabel --> TypeB".
- if an edge ends with "*" after the arrow, it denotes an optional property (after adding the edge, the graph doesn't
meet the support property).
- predicate_uris lists predicate URIs aligned with the edges.
- Attributes marked with "*" are below the subgraph's support threshold (optional attributes).

### Task:
First, assess whether this subgraph allows for meaningful questions. If the subgraph is too trivial, lacks interesting
relationships (eg, graph metadata as opposed to actual knowledge), or doesn't support generating useful and meaningful
questions, set is_meaningful to false and return an empty questions list. 
Otherwise, if you can see at some meaningful concepts and relationsips to elecit a a meaningful and high utility question then return True.

If you set is_meaningful to true, generate 2-4 question-query pairs as a JSON array where each item has:
- text_query: the natural language question
- sparql_query: the corresponding SPARQL query


### Requirements:

#### A. Generation Task
1. Generate 2-4 question-query pairs depending on your assessment of the subgraph's richness. Try to attain high
coverage of all the meaningful aspects of the subgraph in the question-query pairs.
{% if focus_entity_qa %}
2. Great questions are often centered around interesting entities in the graph (entities with a proper natural language label/name or specific real-world referent — not blank nodes, meaningless identifiers, or anonymous instances). All of the questions you produce
must be entity centric. Meanwhile, set is_meaningful to False if no interesting entities are available.
3. You must make the 2-4 questions diverse in style (what, who, whom, whose, when, which, yes/no (ASK) questions, how
compounds, aggregate statistics, superlatives/comparative, etc.) and complexity while ultimately maintaining quality,
meaningfulness and utility of the questions.
{% else %}
2. Whenever there is an interesting entity in the graph (one with a proper natural language label/name or specific real-world referent as opposed to a meaningless identifier/uri), it's good to have a question or two specifically about it. 
3. You must make the 2-4 questions diverse in style (what, who, whom, whose, when, which, yes/no (ASK) questions, how
compounds, aggregate statistics, superlatives/comparative, etc.) and complexity while ultimately maintaining quality,
meaningfulness and utility of the questions.
{% endif %}

#### B. Natural Language Question Quality:
4. Questions must be grounded exclusively in the meaningful classes, entities, relationships, and attributes present in this YAML
subgraph. Never use uninformative knowledge graph specific terminology (generic properties, types, etc.), mention URIs/IDs/prefixes, or reference the
YAML/subgraph in the natural language question — each question and query must be fully self-contained.
5. The phrasing of the natural language question should always be natural, fluent and not awkward or stilted. Question
should always end with a question mark. If a concept term is awkward in the graph, and you are confident it can be rephrased naturally
without being inaccurate, do so.
6. Questions should be certainly answerable and avoid ontology/schema/documentation questions. Focus on questions that
are interesting and meaningful (avoid meaningless questions like "what/how many entities are linked to.../useless patterns...").
7. Focus on questions that would be of high utility to the people in the domain. Never produce a low utility question to avoid being penalized!

#### C. SPARQL Accuracy & Question-Query Alignment:
8. SPARQL queries must be stand-alone executable against a SPARQL endpoint containing the full graph. Declare meaningful
prefixes in the query based on the URIs in the YAML.
9. Use proper SPARQL syntax to generate valid queries. Your SPARQL query must produce exactly the results expected by
the text query; not more and not less. Before finalizing each pair, verify: does the SPARQL query contain every constraint stated in the
question, and no constraints absent from the question?
10. If the question does not have one answer/result, you may optionally use LIMIT in SELECT queries (e.g., LIMIT 100) to
avoid too many results. BUT MAKE SURE to REFLECT THIS in the text query!

### Crucial Notes:
The ultimate goal is to produce high-quality, diverse, and very meaningful questions together with their corresponding
accurate SPARQL queries that can be executed against the full knowledge graph.

An expert in the knowledge graph should be able to reproduce the same sparql query when given text question only.
- You will be rewarded when you generate high-utility questions an expert in the domain would ask where the sparql query is 
perfectly aligned to the question and accurately answering it.
- You will be penalized if the generated SPARQL query and the text question do not properly align (eg, the sparql query
has constraints unrequested in the question... or misses constraints from the question)
- You will be also highly penalized if the generated SPARQL query incorrectly answer the text question or provides no
answer when there is one.
- You will be penalized if your question styles are not diverse enough, if questions are of low quality or low utility or are phrased
awkwardly.
- Flagging the subgraph as not meaningful when it's hard to generate high quality questions is better than generating
low quality questions.
- Generating fewer high quality and high utility questions that a DOMAIN EXPERT may ask is MUCH better than generating more low quality
questions. Quality over quantity.